## Look and feel

Every surface is built from one set of tokens (`uitheme.h`), so the menu, the
settings, the panels and the dialogs read as one app:

| role | colour |
| --- | --- |
| backdrop | `#0b0e11` |
| cards / dialogs / menus | `#141a1f` |
| inputs / buttons | `#1b2229` |
| hairlines | `#2b3540` |
| text | `#e8edf2` |
| secondary text | `#8d9aa7` |
| accent (primary action, selection) | `#00e05a` |
| danger (delete) | `#ff5f56` |

* **Midnight terminal scheme**: backdrop `#0b0e11`, foreground `#d7e0ea`, the
  sixteen ANSI colours retuned so nothing glares on a 720x720 panel; the
  green/blue/yellow entries sit in the same hue family as the UI accent.
* **One font**: the bundled Maple Mono NF CN subset is used for both the
  terminal and the chrome, so Chinese text and commands stop switching faces.
* **Radii and sizes**: cards 18 px, buttons 14 px, menu items 10 px; nothing
  tappable is shorter than 58 px.
* **Three button weights**: solid green for the primary action (run / connect /
  save / close), outlined for the secondary ones, red text for delete.
* **Two-line list rows**: name on top, command or `user@host:port` below in
  mono, elided when long; the selected row grows a green bar on the left.
* **Settings scroll** and split into "connection" / "appearance" cards, each
  option with a one-line explanation.
* **The dropped-connection strip** is a rounded amber card now, not a red box.

## Quick commands and SSH hosts (swipe-down menu -> Cmds / SSH)

The swipe-down menu now has two buttons: **设置** (settings) / **命令 / SSH**.
All on-screen UI text (menu, settings window, key-binding help) is in Chinese; the
donations button and its in-app purchase code have been removed.

### Commands

* Tap a row to select it, then **Run** (text + Enter) or **Insert** (text only,
  so it can still be edited). Double-tap a row to edit it; **Add** / **Delete**
  manage the list.
* A few samples are seeded on the first start (`ls -l`, `df -h`, `ps`,
  `ifconfig`, `uname -a`) - delete what you do not need.
* The text is written straight into the pty, so it behaves exactly like typing:
  it is echoed and lands in the shell history.

### SSH hosts

* Name / user / host / port / key / password. **Connect** is the same as typing
  `ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new -p <port> -i <key> user@host` and
  Enter. The flag is what removes the `Are you sure you want to continue
  connecting (yes/no)?` step on a first connection: a new host is accepted and
  recorded in `data/.ssh/known_hosts`, while a **changed** key is still rejected.
* **Password**: when one is saved, the widget watches the pty output and answers
  the first `password:` prompt (30 s window), then forgets it.

### What saving a password really means

BB10 exposes no system keychain to third-party apps, so the password lives in
QSettings in the app's private data dir (key `SshHosts`) and is only XOR+Base64
obfuscated - **not encrypted**. Anyone with root, a backup of the app data or
the device unlocked can recover it.

Keys are therefore the recommended way:

```sh
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f data/.ssh/id_ed25519      # bundled
```

ssh-agent / ssh-add are not bundled (only ssh, scp, sftp and ssh-keygen are);
a key without a passphrase works directly, a protected one asks every time.

Put the key path in the host entry and leave the password empty; ssh will ask
when it really needs a passphrase.

### Bundled ssh suite

ssh / scp / sftp / ssh-keygen ship inside the bar and land in the app folder:

```
app/native/bin/ssh  scp  sftp  ssh-keygen      ~6.9 MB
app/native/lib/libcrypto.so.3  libz.so.1      ~4.0 MB
```

* `main.cpp` prepends `app/native/bin` to PATH and re-applies the exec bit to the
  four binaries; `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` already contains `app/native/lib`.
* BB10 only has `libcrypto.so.2` / `libz.so.2`, different names from the `.so.3` /
  `.so.1` shipped here, so nothing shadows a system library - and openssh asks
  for exactly those two names, which is why they have to travel with the app.
* The price is a 6.4 MB -> 11.3 MB bar. Do not want it? Drop the `sshbin` assets
  from `bar-descriptor.xml` and repackage.

## Touch gestures

The display handles finger input itself, so nothing has to be selected with a
long-press-then-release dance on a 3.1" screen:

| gesture | what it does |
|---|---|
| tap | puts the keyboard focus back on the terminal |
| long press (0.45 s) | selects the whole line under the finger and opens a small menu: **复制本行** / **复制选中文字** / **复制可见屏幕** / **粘贴** |
| long press + drag | grows the selection to an arbitrary range; the menu item **复制选中文字** copies exactly that |
| two fingers, drag up/down | walks the scrollback one line per line-height |
| two fingers, pinch | font size, 10-60 px, applied immediately and stored in QSettings |

Copied text is trimmed of the blank padding the screen carries, so pasting into
an editor or a shell does not leave trailing spaces. The pinch changes the same
`FontSize` setting as the drop-down in Settings, so the two never disagree.

## Key bindings (Term50-style, no soft-key bar)

The old on-screen button bar (Ctrl+ / Tab / < / > / ^ / v / Esc) is gone: the
terminal now uses the full screen height above the virtual keyboard, and the
keys come from the physical keyboard plus a sticky "CTRL layer" ported from
Term50's metamode scheme.

### Shift is the Ctrl key

BB10 phone keyboards (Q10 / Q30 / Passport / Classic) have **no cursor arrow
keys** - the key printed with an up arrow is the **Shift** key, so that is the
key to use as Ctrl:

* **hold the RIGHT Shift + key** -> Ctrl+key, the real modifier behaviour:
  `c`=Ctrl+C, `d`=Ctrl+D, `z`=Ctrl+Z, `l`=Ctrl+L, `[`=Ctrl+[, ...
  (A-Z and `[ \ ] ^ _` all map to `key - 0x40`; arrows/Home/End/Delete/PgUp/PgDn
  are sent in xterm form `ESC[1;5X`.)
* **double-tap the RIGHT Shift** (within 500 ms, with nothing typed in between)
  -> toggles the sticky CTRL layer; a green `CTRL` badge appears in the
  bottom-right corner while it is armed.
* the **left Shift stays a normal Shift** - it is never turned into Ctrl, so
  uppercase typing and the BB10 shift/SYM workaround are untouched. Typing
  capitals (`Shift`,`A`,`Shift`,`B`, ...) cannot arm the layer, because
  any other key press cancels the pending double tap.
* both Shift keys arrive as `Qt::Key_Shift`; left/right are told apart by the
  QNX keycode (`KEYCODE_RIGHT_SHIFT`) carried in `nativeVirtualKey()` /
  `nativeScanCode()`. If neither carries a keycode (both 0), the two keys
  are indistinguishable and the double tap is accepted from either Shift. If a
  device port reports something unexpected, arm the layer and press `?`: the
  help overlay prints the last Shift `native`/`scan` keycode so the mapping
  can be pinned down.

Inside the CTRL layer:

| key | action |
|-----|--------|
| `e` | Esc |
| `s` | Ctrl+L (rescreen) |
| `v` | paste clipboard |
| `i` / `o` / `z` | font bigger / smaller / reset (saved to QSettings) |
| `r` | reload settings from QSettings |
| `?` | keybinding help overlay |
| `h` `j` `k` `l` | left / down / up / right, stays in the layer |
| arrows | keep moving, stays in the layer (external keyboards) |
| anything else | Ctrl+&lt;key&gt; and leave the layer |

### SYM: the Term50 symmenu overlay

The physical **SYM** key pops up Term50's `main_symmenu` grid (see
`symbolpanel.cpp`) - the same 19 symbols, in the same two rows, that Term50's
`DEFAULT_SYMMENU_ENTRIES` / `DEFAULT_SYMMENU_ROW_LENS {10, 9}` define:

```
 ~    `    {    }    [    ]    <    >    ^    %
 q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p

 =    -    *    /    \    |    &    '    "
 a    s    d    f    g    h    j    k    l
```

Every cell shows the glyph with the key that picks it in the corner (green),
exactly like Term50's renderer. There are two ways to insert a symbol:

* **tap the cell** (Q30 / Passport / Classic touchscreen), or
* **press that key** - the grid owns the keyboard while it is up.

SYM or Esc again closes it; any other key closes it and is swallowed, so a
stray keystroke can never leak into the shell. The grid re-lays itself out on
rotation and never resizes the terminal (no rescreen, unlike Term50).

SYM is detected by its QNX keycode `0xF0D3` (`KEYCODE_BB_SYM_KEY`, Term50
`src/terminal.h`) through `nativeVirtualKey()`/`nativeScanCode()`; firmware
that reports SYM as a bare Escape keeps working through that fallback, since
the phones have no Escape key of their own (Esc is `e` in the CTRL layer).
This replaces the old `myvk` `vk.png` picture, which only *showed* the
symbols and had to be dismissed by pressing a key.
The grid also carries a control row along its bottom edge:

```
 Esc          Tab          Shift+Tab
```

BB10's keyboard has no Tab key at all and Escape is buried in the CTRL layer
(double-tap right Shift, then `e`), so neither is reachable from a terminal
prompt. **Esc** sends `0x1b` and closes the grid (that is what you reach for
Esc for); **Tab** sends `0x09` and **Shift+Tab** sends `ESC [ Z`, and both keep
the grid up so it can be tapped a few times in a row before SYM / Esc folds it
away again.

### 中文输入法: pinyin input inside the terminal

BB10's own input panel is no help for a terminal (the pty needs the raw
UTF-8, and Qt4 on BB10 has no input method plugin a full screen widget can
drive), so the IME runs inside the widget, exactly like Term50's:

* **Alt+Enter** (or **Ctrl+Space**) switches 中文 / EN. A green **中** badge
  sits in the top left corner while it is on, and the state is remembered
  across restarts.
* **a-z** type pinyin, **Space** takes the highlighted candidate, **1-8**
  take the candidate with that number, **- , <** and **= . >** page — but
  only while the strip really has more than one page. With a single page
  they are plain punctuation: the letters typed so far go out first and the
  shell then gets the `,` itself, so `hello, world` keeps its comma.
  **Esc** drops the composition without sending anything.
* **Enter** does not take a candidate: while composing it commits the
  letters you typed as plain English (`Hello` -> `Hello`), so typing an
  English word never needs a 中/EN toggle. Nothing is appended - no space
  and no newline - so the composition simply ends and a second Enter (with
  nothing being composed) runs the line. With nothing being composed it is
  the normal Enter. A key the engine cannot use
  (punctuation, a function key) ends the composition the same way, with
  the letters kept, so `Hello!` really sends `Hello!`.
* the candidate strip along the bottom edge shows the current page:

  ```
  [中]  nihao   1 你好   2 你   3 尼   4 泥      1/1  < >
  ```

  Every candidate can be tapped as well, so the phone keyboard alone is
  enough - no external keyboard needed.

  On a **Q20 / Q10 / Q5 / Classic** (no touchscreen, a trackpad instead) the
  trackpad drives the strip: left-right steps the green frame from candidate to
  candidate, up-down turns the page, and a trackpad click commits the framed
  one. The arrow keys on an external keyboard do the same. The steps are paced
  like a key repeat (80 ms), because the trackpad streams one event per pixel.
  While no composition is running the trackpad keeps its old job and sends
  arrow keys to the shell.

  A word candidate is two or three cells wide, so a page shows only as many as
  fit; the rest of that page stays reachable with the number keys and the
  paging keys.
* whole words work, not just one character at a time: `nihao` -> 你好,
  `zhongwen` -> 中文, `xianzai` -> 现在, and an unknown word falls back to
  syllable by syllable (`woaini` -> 我爱你).
* the candidate you pick is remembered (`$HOME/.term/cjk_ime_freq`), so the
  characters you use most come first the next time - and the shipped order is
  frequency ordered too (`tools/order-ime-dict.py`), otherwise typing `shi`
  would start with 世 事 仕 似 instead of 是.

The engine is Term50's `cjk_ime.c` / `cjk_ime_dict.inc`, copied in unchanged
(only `extern "C"` was added to the header so C++ can call it) - 8230 single
characters plus ~28k words.

8105 is every character of 《通用规范汉字表》. GB2312, what older phones and PCs
could produce, has 125 characters that table dropped - mostly radicals people
do type (氵 扌 艹 讠 纟 钅 饣 辶 阝 灬 忄 疒 礻 衤) and a few odd ones (磺 矽 捱
搿 渖 飚 齄). `tools/add-gb2312-chars.py` adds them with the readings from the
pinyin-data dump in `tools/cache`, so all 6763 GB2312 hanzi can now be typed
and every one of them has a glyph.

Known hole: 273 of the 8105 are 繁体/rare characters outside the BMP (Ext-A,
Ext-B/C, e.g. 㑇 䁖 𠅤) - Maple Mono has no glyph for those blocks at all, so
picking one from the strip writes a character that shows up as a box. They are
last in their reading, and no phrase uses them. Rendering needs glyphs the device stock fonts do
not have, so `main.cpp` registers the bundled `assets/bgscjk.ttf` (a subset of
the kit's Maple Mono NF CN covering the dictionary) at start up and uses that
family for the terminal: Chinese is exactly two cells wide, as `Screen.cpp`
assumes. Adjust the strip in `imepanel.cpp`, the key mapping in
`TerminalDisplay::handleImeKey`.

The engine is plain C with no Qt in it, so its behaviour is tested on the build
host: `tools/test-cjk-ime.sh` builds `cjk_ime.c` with the host compiler and
checks the words, the candidate order, the paging, the commit and the keys that
have to keep reaching the shell (21 checks). Run it after touching the
dictionary - `tools/order-ime-dict.py` and `tools/gen-ime-data.py` (font
subset) are the other two tools worth re-running.

Two widget-level checks run on the host as well, with no NDK involved:
`tests/run-shift-layer-check.sh` (left Shift stays Shift, the Ctrl layer comes
from the right one) and `tests/run-ime-page-check.sh` (when `,` `.` `-` `[` `]`
`=` page the candidates and when they stay plain punctuation). Both compile code
lifted verbatim out of `TerminalDisplay.cpp`, so they cannot drift away from
what ships.

Other chords: **Alt+Enter = 中文/EN**, **Ctrl+Space = the same**,
**Alt+Space = Tab**, **Shift+Alt+key = Ctrl+key**, and double-tapping the
physical Right arrow also toggles the layer (external keyboards only - the
phones have no arrow keys). Term50 puts `ime_toggle` on Alt+Space; here it
moved to Alt+Enter and Alt+Space went back to Tab, because the phone
keyboards have no Tab key and completion matters more.

Term50 bindings with no BBShell equivalent are intentionally absent:
`tab_new/tab_next/tab_prev/tab_close` (single-session app), `url_pick`,
`reload_config` (there is no `.term.lua`; `r` re-reads QSettings instead),
and the Lua/termctl surfaces.

Tunables: `META_DOUBLE_TAP_MS` and `META_DEFAULT_FONT_SIZE` at the top of the
key-layer block in `TerminalDisplay.cpp`.
